Instead of a static function that only calculates the time once when the modal opens, we can turn it into a tiny, self-updating React component.
Here is exactly how to make that timeline completely dynamic in **VoucherPostingStatus.js**:
### 1. Create the Live Timer Component
Find where you pasted the calculateTimeElapsed function from the previous step. **Delete it**, and replace it with this LiveTimeElapsed React component.
*(Place this right above your export default function VoucherPostingStatus() { line)*
```javascript
// --- LIVE TICKING TIMER COMPONENT ---
const LiveTimeElapsed = ({ startTime }) => {
  const [timeString, setTimeString] = useState("0h 0m");

  useEffect(() => {
    const calculate = () => {
      if (!startTime) return "0h 0m";
      const diffMins = Math.floor(
        Math.abs(new Date() - new Date(startTime)) / 60000
      );
      const hours = Math.floor(diffMins / 60);
      const mins = diffMins % 60;
      return `${hours}h ${mins}m`;
    };

    // Calculate immediately on mount
    setTimeString(calculate());

    // Update the string dynamically every 60 seconds
    const interval = setInterval(() => {
      setTimeString(calculate());
    }, 60000);

    return () => clearInterval(interval);
  }, [startTime]);

  return <>{timeString}</>;
};

```
